# =====================================================================
# sem-reader.media.prompt — UX design sheet for the sem-reader reading
# chrome. Reference images come from the per-tag snapshot run:
#   /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-reader/
# (harness names each capture <example>-<view>-<width>.png; see index.md
# there for the exact list). Do not invent modes: the reader's modes are
# exactly the ones enumerated below — chrome states plus the effect of
# each control. One example page per mode, same document in every frame.
# =====================================================================
schema: '0.4'

id: sem-reader-ux-modes
type: image
quality: high

prompt:
  text: |
    A UX design sheet for "sem-reader", the reading chrome of a long
    technical document (outline, reading-progress bar, and reader-side
    controls: focus mode, type size and face, colour scheme, print,
    audience). The sheet shows the SAME document — a runbook titled
    "Token rotation runbook" with sections "Before you start", "Run the
    rotation", "After" — rendered once per display mode, arranged as a
    labelled grid of browser-page mockups on one board. Visual language:
    calm minimal dark documentation UI (near-black canvas, warm
    off-white text, one restrained accent), system-serif body option,
    no marketing flourishes.

    Frames to include, one per mode, each clearly captioned:

    1. Chrome collapsed (default) — a slim horizontal reading bar above
       the document: a "Contents" button on the left, small preference
       controls (focus, type down/up, serif toggle, colour select,
       print) on the right, and a hairline progress bar along the bar's
       bottom edge about one third filled. Document body fully readable
       below.
    2. Outline panel open — the same bar with a panel beneath it
       listing the document's h2 sections as links, nested h3 entries
       indented; the link for the section currently in view carries a
       visible current-position marker (accent dot or underline), the
       others plain. The panel overlays only the top of the page; the
       document stays visible behind and below it.
    3. Authored contents nav — same open-panel composition, but the
       list is visibly hand-authored (different link wording, deeper
       nesting), demonstrating that the outline can be supplied by the
       author rather than generated.
    4. Focus mode on — the page chrome outside the article column
       (site header, sidebars, footers) dimmed to near-invisible while
       the article column and the reading bar stay at full contrast;
       text itself unchanged.
    5. Type scale — three small side-by-side frames labelled s / m / l
       showing the same paragraph at the three sizes; controls' minus
       and plus buttons visibly disabled at the s and l ends.
    6. Serif face — the same paragraph once in the sans face, once in
       the serif face, the serif button in a pressed state.
    7. Colour scheme — the document shown in dark and in light; the
       reading bar and outline restyle with it, contrast maintained in
       both; a small "auto / light / dark" select rendered on the bar.
    8. Print — a paper-white A4 page mockup: no reading bar, no
       progress, no controls; just the document with its outline as a
       plain table of contents at the top.
    9. Audience — the reading bar carrying an "Audience" select
       offering Everyone, Reader, Operator; the document beneath
       unchanged (selection is applied elsewhere, the control itself
       just reflects the choice).
    10. Mobile 390 — the bar compressed to "Contents" plus an overflow
        control, progress hairline retained, outline panel as a full
        width sheet from the top.

    Composition: captions small and technical; frames aligned on a
    common grid; the reading bar identical in every applicable frame
    except for the state being demonstrated. Flat UI mockup style,
    crisp text, no browser chrome ornament beyond a simple title bar.
  negative: photorealistic screenshot collage, marketing hero, gradient
    banners, stock-photo people, decorative illustrations, watermark,
    embedded captions outside the frame grid, cluttered spacing,
    illegible placeholder gibbering text in body copy
  style: flat vector UI mockup, precise 1px hairlines, generous
    whitespace, documentation-site aesthetic

attachments:
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-reader/in-document-render-1280.png
    role: reference
    description: actual rendered reference page with the reader chrome in the collapsed state
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-reader/in-document-render-390.png
    role: reference
    description: mobile rendering of the same page for the mobile frame

output:
  formats:
    - format: png
  dimensions:
    width: 1536
    height: 1024
    aspect_ratio: '3:2'

requirements:
  subject: UX mode sheet for a document reading-chrome element
  composition: labelled grid of browser-page mockups, one per mode, same document throughout
  palette: near-black canvas with warm off-white text and one restrained accent; light mode frames paper-white
  do:
    - keep body text fully legible in every frame
    - show the progress bar as a hairline, never a banner
    - keep control labels literal: Contents, focus, type, colour, print, audience
    - maintain readable contrast in both colour modes
  dont:
    - never occlude or blur body text (occlusion is sem-spoiler's job, not the reader's)
    - never invent controls outside the enumerated set (no bookmark, share, AI buttons)
    - no modal dialogs, no cookie banners, no ads
    - no emoji, no decorative icons replacing text labels

eval:
  pass_threshold: 0.75
  required_pass:
    - mode_coverage
    - text_legibility
    - control_accuracy
  criteria:
    mode_coverage:
      weight: 3
      description: all ten enumerated modes present as distinct captioned frames
      fail_signals:
        - missing outline panel frame
        - missing print frame
        - missing mobile frame
    text_legibility:
      weight: 3
      description: body text readable in every frame; focus mode dims chrome, never the article
      fail_signals:
        - blurred or gibberish body text
        - article text dimmed in focus-mode frame
    control_accuracy:
      weight: 2
      description: only the enumerated controls appear, correctly labelled and ordered
      fail_signals:
        - invented controls such as share or bookmark
        - progress bar rendered as a large filled banner
  reject_if:
    - watermark, logo, or signature
    - body text occluded or replaced by placeholder stripes
    - photorealistic collage rather than flat UI mockup

tags:
  - semtext
  - sem-reader
  - ux-design-sheet
product_targets: []
